    "canonicalStory": "Apple began in a garage and grew with unusual speed. Steve Jobs became the public face of a company changing personal computing, but growth also magnified conflict. In 1985, after a struggle over strategy and management, Jobs lost his operational role and left the company he had co-founded. The loss was intensely personal. Apple was not merely an employer. It was the project around which Jobs had built his adult identity. Public success had been followed by public rejection. Jobs did not disappear. He founded NeXT, a computer company whose products struggled commercially but developed advanced software. He also acquired the computer graphics group that became Pixar. Pixar endured years of uncertainty before \"Toy Story\" transformed it into an animation leader. Meanwhile, Apple struggled. In 1996, Apple acquired NeXT, bringing its software---and Jobs---back into the company. The technology developed during the exile became part of the foundation for Apple's future operating systems. The years away had also exposed Jobs to new industries, new collaborators, and the discipline of rebuilding. His return did not erase the firing. It made the firing part of the architecture of the comeback. Apple later introduced products that reshaped music, phones, and personal technology. This story should not be simplified into \"getting fired is good.\" It can be devastating. The deeper point is that rejection can separate a person from a title without separating them from their capacity. Jobs lost his position at Apple. He did not lose the ability to create, learn, or return with something the company eventually needed.",
    "reelspiration": "A title can be taken from you. Your ability to keep creating cannot.",
    "principle": "Career setbacks can become capability-building seasons when you continue producing assets.",
    "nextStep": "List what you can still build, learn, or own even if your current role disappeared tomorrow.",
    "whyItMatters": "Jobs's years away from Apple produced NeXT and helped develop Pixar. NeXT technology later became foundational to Apple's modern operating systems, while Pixar transformed animation. The period that looked like exile became part of the infrastructure for his return and for products that reshaped personal technology.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Steve Jobs was forced out of Apple---the company he helped create. For\nmost people, losing a job is painful. Imagine losing the company that\nhad become part of your identity. Jobs could not control the decision,\nbut he could control what he built next. He founded NeXT. He helped turn\nPixar into an animation company. Years later, Apple acquired NeXT and\nbrought him back. The technology created during his exile helped support\nApple's next generation, and the experiences changed the leader who\nreturned. THE REELSPIRATION: A title can be taken from you. Your ability\nto keep creating cannot.",
    "carouselAuthored": "-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **1**                               He was pushed out of the company he\n                                      co-founded.\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **2**                               Apple had become more than a\n                                      job---it was part of Steve Jobs's\n                                      identity.\n\n  **3**                               Leaving was public, painful, and\n                                      beyond his control.\n\n  **4**                               He founded NeXT and helped build\n                                      Pixar through years of uncertainty.\n\n  **5**                               Apple later acquired NeXT---and\n                                      Jobs returned.\n\n  **6**                               The work from his exile helped\n                                      support Apple's future.\n\n  **7**                               THE REELSPIRATION: A position can\n                                      be taken. Your capacity to create\n                                      remains yours.\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------",
    "linkedInAuthored": "The common version of the Steve Jobs story celebrates the comeback. The\nmore useful business lesson is what happened in the middle. NeXT was not\na conventional commercial triumph, yet its software became strategically\nvaluable. Pixar required patience before it became transformative.\nCareers and ventures often produce assets before they produce obvious\nsuccess: technology, judgment, relationships, perspective, and\ncredibility. A period that looks unsuccessful by one metric may be\ncreating the exact capability required for the next chapter.",
    "visualDirection": "13. Scene 1: Garage-era computer components\n\n14. Scene 2: Boardroom doors closing\n\n15. Scene 3: Founder walking alone through a dark corridor\n\n16. Scene 4: Black NeXT cube and lines of code\n\n17. Scene 5: Early animation workstation lights\n\n18. Scene 6: Apple stage silhouette returning to bright light",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Avoid portraying Jobs as a flawless leader. Focus the story narrowly on\nresilience and capability development. \"Fired\" is a useful shorthand,\nbut the corporate struggle was complex; use \"forced out\" or \"lost his\noperational role\" in factual long-form copy.",
    "sourceId": "RS-B003",
