    "canonicalStory": "James Dyson was frustrated by a common problem: as a vacuum bag filled with dust, suction dropped. The appliance still looked functional, but its performance deteriorated. Dyson wondered whether cyclonic separation---technology he had seen used industrially---could remove dust without a disposable bag. The idea was easy to describe and difficult to engineer. Dyson began building prototypes. They did not work well enough. He adjusted dimensions, airflow, materials, and internal geometry, then tested again. Four years and 5,127 prototypes later, he had a workable cyclonic vacuum design. The number matters not because persistence is automatically noble, but because each version represented a question answered in physical form. Major manufacturers rejected the concept. A bagless vacuum threatened an industry that also earned money from replacement bags. Dyson eventually pursued the product independently. In 1993, the DC01 launched in the United Kingdom, and the company grew around the idea that everyday products could be reconsidered through engineering. The finished product hides the uncertainty that produced it. Consumers see a clean object. They do not see the failed seals, wrong airflows, disappointing tests, financial pressure, or the thousands of versions that never reached a shelf. Dyson's story reframes failure. A failed prototype is not necessarily a verdict. If it produces information and changes the next attempt, it is part of the design process. The danger is not that version one fails. The danger is repeating version one without learning.",
    "reelspiration": "Failure is useful when it leaves the next attempt smarter than the last.",
    "principle": "Iteration compounds only when every attempt produces information.",
    "nextStep": "Choose one current failure and write the specific variable you will change in the next attempt.",
    "whyItMatters": "Dyson's persistence helped turn cyclonic separation into a major consumer technology platform and built a company centered on engineering-led iteration. His story gave entrepreneurs a visible model for treating failed prototypes as research rather than personal verdicts.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "James Dyson did not create one vacuum prototype and get it right. He\nbuilt 5,127. For years, he changed shapes, airflow, materials, and\ndetails---then tested again. Major manufacturers rejected the bagless\nidea. But every failed version answered a question, and every answer\nchanged the next build. Prototype 5,127 became the foundation of a\nglobal engineering company. The polished product made the journey look\ninevitable. It was not. It was accumulated learning disguised as\npersistence. THE REELSPIRATION: Failure is useful when it leaves the\nnext attempt smarter than the last.",
    "carouselAuthored": "-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **1**                               The breakthrough arrived on\n                                      prototype 5,127.\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **2**                               James Dyson wanted to solve the\n                                      suction loss caused by clogged\n                                      vacuum bags.\n\n  **3**                               The idea required years of physical\n                                      experiments.\n\n  **4**                               Most prototypes failed to meet the\n                                      goal.\n\n  **5**                               Major manufacturers still rejected\n                                      the finished concept.\n\n  **6**                               Dyson launched independently and\n                                      built a global engineering brand.\n\n  **7**                               THE REELSPIRATION: A failed attempt\n                                      is progress when it teaches the\n                                      next attempt what to change.\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------",
    "linkedInAuthored": "Dyson's 5,127 prototypes are often used as a persistence slogan. The\noperational lesson is stronger: iteration needs instrumentation.\nRepetition alone does not create progress. Each test must isolate\nsomething, produce information, and influence the next design.\nBusinesses can apply the same discipline to offers, landing pages,\npricing, workflows, and products. Do not merely \"keep trying.\" Preserve\nthe learning architecture: hypothesis, test, observation, change.",
    "visualDirection": "19. Scene 1: Dust clogging a vacuum bag in macro detail\n\n20. Scene 2: Cardboard cyclone prototype in a workshop\n\n21. Scene 3: Rapid sequence of numbered prototypes\n\n22. Scene 4: Rejection letters and closed factory gates\n\n23. Scene 5: Final transparent cyclone airflow visualization\n\n24. Scene 6: Modern engineering lab filled with prototypes",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Dyson's own materials state 5,127 prototypes over four years.\nDistinguish between 5,126 unsuccessful development versions and the\nsuccessful 5,127th. Do not imply every prototype was a complete vacuum.",
    "sourceId": "RS-B004",
    "pattern": "Iteration • Prototype Failure • Inventor",
