    "slug": "jan-koum",
    "subject": "Jan Koum",
    "dek": "From Food Stamps to a Product Built Around Simplicity",
    "challenges": [
      "starting-over"
    ],
    "collections": [
      "business-builders",
      "entrepreneurs"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Jan Koum immigrated to the United States from Ukraine as a teenager with his mother. Money was limited, and the family relied on public assistance while adapting to a new country. Koum taught himself about computer networking and eventually worked at Yahoo, where he met Brian Acton. After leaving, the two explored new opportunities. Koum began developing an application built around mobile status updates, then shifted toward messaging as smartphone notifications improved. WhatsApp was initially unstable and attracted limited enthusiasm. Koum reportedly considered abandoning it. Acton encouraged him to continue. The product evolved around a disciplined promise: fast, simple communication without advertising clutter. The founders resisted many conventional growth tactics. WhatsApp spread because it solved a universal problem with little friction---reaching people across devices and borders. Facebook acquired the company in 2014 for a deal valued at approximately $19 billion at announcement. The dramatic distance between public assistance and a multibillion-dollar acquisition makes the story memorable. The more useful insight is the product discipline in the middle. WhatsApp did not win by trying to become everything. It became valuable by doing a basic human job reliably. Hardship does not automatically produce clarity, but it can create respect for simplicity. When resources are limited, unnecessary complexity becomes easier to recognize.",
    "reelspiration": "Your beginning may limit your resources, but it does not limit the reach of a useful idea.",
    "principle": "Simplicity becomes strategic when it protects the customer's core need.",
    "nextStep": "Remove one feature, step, or message that distracts from the single job your product should do best.",
    "whyItMatters": "WhatsApp helped normalize simple, internet-based global messaging across borders and devices. Koum's sensitivity to unreliable communication and privacy shaped a product that connected families, workers, and communities at enormous scale.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Jan Koum arrived in the United States from Ukraine as a teenager. His\nfamily struggled financially and relied on public assistance. Years\nlater, he began building WhatsApp. The early app was unstable, and Koum\nconsidered giving up. His co-founder, Brian Acton, urged him to keep\ngoing. They focused on something simple: reliable messaging without\nunnecessary clutter. WhatsApp spread across borders and was eventually\nacquired in a deal valued at about \\$19 billion when announced. THE\nREELSPIRATION: Your beginning may limit your resources, but it does not\nlimit the reach of a useful idea.",
    "carouselAuthored": "-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **1**                               He arrived in America as a teenager\n                                      with very little.\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **2**                               Jan Koum's family relied on public\n                                      assistance while starting over.\n\n  **3**                               Years later, he began building a\n                                      simple mobile communication tool.\n\n  **4**                               The early version struggled, and he\n                                      considered quitting.\n\n  **5**                               He and Brian Acton kept refining\n                                      the product around reliable\n                                      messaging.\n\n  **6**                               WhatsApp connected people across\n                                      borders and devices.\n\n  **7**                               THE REELSPIRATION: Scarcity can\n                                      teach you to remove everything that\n                                      does not serve the real need.\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------",
    "linkedInAuthored": "WhatsApp is often framed as a rags-to-riches story. Product leaders\nshould also notice what the company refused to become. It prioritized\nfast, cross-border communication and minimized distractions. Focus is\nnot merely choosing what to build; it is repeatedly defending the\ncentral job from attractive additions. Companies with fewer resources\ncan compete by making fewer promises and keeping them exceptionally\nwell.",
    "visualDirection": "43. Scene 1: Teen and mother arriving with suitcases\n\n44. Scene 2: Grocery line and assistance paperwork shown respectfully\n\n45. Scene 3: Computer networking books and late-night study\n\n46. Scene 4: Early smartphone interface failing and restarting\n\n47. Scene 5: Clean message bubbles crossing a world map\n\n48. Scene 6: Simple connection line between distant families",
