    "collections": [
      "business-builders",
      "entrepreneurs"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Jack Ma grew up in Hangzhou, China, and developed English skills by offering tours to foreign visitors. He failed important exams more than once and has publicly described being rejected from many jobs. Among the best-known stories is his account of applying to a newly opened KFC in Hangzhou and being the only applicant in his group not hired. The anecdote became symbolic because it placed an ordinary rejection beside the company he later built. Ma encountered the internet during a trip to the United States and became convinced it would create opportunities for Chinese businesses. His early internet venture, China Pages, struggled. Later, he gathered a group in his apartment and founded Alibaba to help businesses connect with buyers. The company entered a market with powerful competitors, limited trust in online commerce, and infrastructure that was still developing. Alibaba survived by focusing on local conditions, building services around the ecosystem, and maintaining a longer horizon than many observers expected. Ma's public story contains many memorable rejection counts, and some have been repeated without careful sourcing. Reelspiration should not depend on the arithmetic. The reliable pattern is enough: traditional institutions repeatedly failed to select him, while he continued learning, teaching, selling, and organizing people around a new opportunity. Rejection is often interpreted as objective evaluation. In reality, it may measure fit with a specific role, at a specific time, under someone else's criteria. Entrepreneurship creates a different test: can you organize value where no role yet exists?",
    "reelspiration": "Rejection can close someone else's door without measuring what you are capable of building outside it.",
    "principle": "External selection is contextual, not a complete measure of potential.",
    "nextStep": "After your next rejection, write what the decision actually measured---and what it did not.",
    "whyItMatters": "Alibaba helped expand digital commerce infrastructure for businesses and consumers in China and beyond. Ma's story became globally influential because it reframed rejection as a temporary response---not a permanent measurement of capability.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Jack Ma has spoken about failing exams and being rejected from\njobs---including a KFC application in which he said everyone but him was\nhired. Later, his first internet venture struggled. But Ma had seen\nsomething others in his market had not yet fully recognized: the\ninternet could connect Chinese businesses with the world. He gathered a\nsmall group in an apartment and founded Alibaba. The institutions that\nrejected him were selecting for existing roles. He went on to create a\nnew one. THE REELSPIRATION: Rejection can close someone else's door\nwithout measuring what you are capable of building outside it.",
    "carouselAuthored": "-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **1**                               He was repeatedly rejected from\n                                      conventional opportunities.\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **2**                               Jack Ma has said a local KFC hired\n                                      every applicant in his group except\n                                      him.\n\n  **3**                               His first internet venture also\n                                      struggled.\n\n  **4**                               But he believed the internet could\n                                      connect Chinese businesses to\n                                      global buyers.\n\n  **5**                               He gathered a small team and\n                                      founded Alibaba in an apartment.\n\n  **6**                               The rejected applicant became the\n                                      builder of a new marketplace.\n\n  **7**                               THE REELSPIRATION: A \"no\" measures\n                                      one opportunity---not the size of\n                                      your potential.\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------",
    "linkedInAuthored": "The most useful version of Jack Ma's rejection story is not \"rejection\npredicts greatness.\" Most rejected people will not build global\ncompanies. The strategic lesson is that selection systems assess\ncandidates against known roles. Emerging markets often require\ncapabilities those systems do not yet recognize: evangelizing a new\nbehavior, assembling trust, and persisting before infrastructure is\nmature. When the role does not exist, the entrepreneur's work is to\ncreate both the opportunity and the proof.",
    "visualDirection": "49. Scene 1: Young guide practicing English near a lake\n\n50. Scene 2: Exam paper and job application stamps\n\n51. Scene 3: KFC storefront viewed from outside without logo emphasis\n\n52. Scene 4: Early internet café and slow-loading webpage\n\n53. Scene 5: Small apartment meeting around a table\n\n54. Scene 6: Digital marketplace lines connecting small businesses",
