    "collections": [
      "athletic-adversity",
      "athletes"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Wilma Rudolph entered the world early, small, and medically fragile. As a child in segregated Tennessee, she battled repeated illness, including pneumonia and polio. The disease weakened her left leg, and for years she relied on a metal brace and regular treatment. Her family helped carry the burden: her mother traveled long distances for medical care, while siblings assisted with exercises at home. The transformation did not happen in one dramatic moment. It happened through patient repetition. Stretching. Therapy. Pain. Small gains that were almost invisible from one day to the next. Eventually, Rudolph stopped wearing the brace. Then she began walking without it. Then running. Basketball first revealed her speed. Track refined it. As a teenager, she qualified for the 1956 Olympics and earned a bronze medal as part of a relay team. That achievement alone would have been extraordinary for the child once told walking might be difficult. But Rudolph was not finished. At the 1960 Olympic Games in Rome, she won gold in the 100 meters, 200 meters, and 4-by-100-meter relay, becoming the first American woman to win three track-and-field gold medals at a single Olympics. Her grace and speed earned her the nickname \"The Tornado\" at home and \"The Black Gazelle\" internationally. Her story is bigger than a medal count. It is the story of progress that began long before anyone was watching. The Olympic finish line was only the public evidence of thousands of private steps.",
    "reelspiration": "The world may celebrate your finish line, but your future is usually built in the steps nobody sees.",
    "principle": "Small, repeated improvements can eventually produce outcomes that once looked impossible.",
    "nextStep": "Choose one ability you want to rebuild. Complete one deliberately small repetition today---and record it.",
    "whyItMatters": "Rudolph's Olympic victories challenged assumptions about disability, illness, race, and women's athletic potential. Her achievements became public proof that progress made quietly over years can alter what a generation believes is possible.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Doctors once feared Wilma Rudolph might never walk normally. Polio\nweakened her left leg, and for years she wore a metal brace. Her\nrecovery was not instant. It was therapy, family support, painful\nexercises, and one small improvement after another. Eventually she\nwalked without the brace. Then she ran. At seventeen, she reached the\nOlympics. Four years later in Rome, she won three gold medals in track\nand field and became known as the fastest woman in the world. Wilma did\nnot leap from limitation to greatness. She built the distance one step\nat a time. Here is today's Reelspiration: The world may celebrate your\nfinish line, but your future is usually built in the steps nobody sees.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- Doctors feared she might never walk normally.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Polio weakened Wilma Rudolph's left leg, and she spent years\nwearing a brace.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Recovery came through treatment, family support, and\nthousands of repeated exercises.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- She learned to walk freely. Then she discovered she could\nrun.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- At 17, she reached the Olympics and earned a relay bronze\nmedal.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- In 1960, she won three Olympic gold medals in track and\nfield.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Your future is often built in the\nsteps nobody sees.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Close shot of a child-sized leg brace in a quiet room.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Family hands assisting with physical-therapy exercises.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Shoes taking tentative steps, then transitioning into\nrunning spikes.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Vintage track lanes and a teenage runner training alone.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Olympic stadium buildup with three race moments.\n\nSCENE 6 --- Final portrait treatment with gold-medal silhouette and\nbranded end card.",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Avoid claiming that one doctor issued a precise lifelong prognosis\nunless the quote is sourced. Present polio, the brace, rehabilitation,\n1956 bronze, and 1960 gold medals with institutional sourcing.",
