    "collections": [
      "athletic-adversity",
      "athletes"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Bethany Hamilton grew up in Hawaii with the ocean at the center of her life. She began competing young, earned sponsorship attention, and imagined a future in professional surfing. Then, on October 31, 2003, a shark attack severed her left arm near the shoulder. The physical loss was immediate. The emotional questions came afterward. Could she paddle strongly enough? Could she balance? Could she still become the athlete she had planned to be? The attack changed her body, but it did not erase her relationship with the water. On November 26---less than a month later---Hamilton returned to surfing. The comeback required adaptation, not denial. She learned new ways to paddle, push up, position her body, and read the wave. Falls were not evidence that she no longer belonged. They were information about the new technique she had to build. Within two years, she won a national title in her age division. She continued competing professionally and later shared her experience through books, films, speaking, and mentorship. Her story traveled far beyond surfing because it showed something universal: returning does not require becoming the person you were before. Sometimes courage means learning who you can become now. Hamilton's faith is central to how she understands her experience and should be represented respectfully when telling her story. The power of the narrative is not that trauma was good. It is that trauma did not receive authority over every chapter that followed.",
    "reelspiration": "Moving forward does not always mean getting your old life back. Sometimes it means discovering how powerful your new life can become.",
    "principle": "Resilience is not pretending nothing changed; it is building effectively from what is true now.",
    "nextStep": "Name one changed circumstance you have been resisting. Write one way you can adapt your method without abandoning your goal.",
    "whyItMatters": "Hamilton's return changed how millions understood adaptation after traumatic loss. Her continued career and public advocacy created a visible reference point for young people facing disability, injury, and sudden change.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "At thirteen, Bethany Hamilton was already building a future in\ncompetitive surfing. Then a shark attack took her left arm. The loss\nchanged every movement required to ride a wave---paddling, balance,\ntiming, and getting to her feet. Less than one month later, Bethany\nreturned to the ocean. Not because everything felt normal, but because\nsurfing was still part of who she was. She rebuilt her technique around\nthe body she had now. Within two years, she won a national title in her\nage division and continued into professional competition. Here is\ntoday's Reelspiration: Moving forward does not always mean getting your\nold life back. Sometimes it means discovering how powerful your new life\ncan become.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- One moment changed her body---and threatened her dream.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- At 13, a shark attack took Bethany Hamilton's left arm.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Every basic movement in surfing had to be relearned.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Less than one month later, she returned to the ocean.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- She adapted her paddling, balance, timing, and technique.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Within two years, she won a national title in her age\ndivision.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Your new path can still lead somewhere\nextraordinary.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Peaceful Hawaiian ocean at dawn.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Young surfer preparing equipment; avoid graphic attack\nimagery.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Empty board and subdued hospital-transition symbolism.\n\nSCENE 4 --- One-arm paddling and repeated attempts from respectful\nangles.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Competition waves and confident turns.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card blending ocean horizon with Reelspiration mark.",
