    "canonicalStory": "Kurt Warner did not enter professional football as a celebrated draft pick. After playing at Northern Iowa, he went undrafted. The Green Bay Packers invited him to training camp in 1994, then released him before the season. With the NFL door closed, Warner worked at a grocery store in Iowa while continuing to train. The image has become famous because of what followed, but the important part is what the job represented: the dream was not paying him, the public was not applauding, and there was no guarantee that effort would lead back to football. Warner joined the Iowa Barnstormers of the Arena Football League, then played in NFL Europe. Those leagues were not consolation prizes to him. They were places to improve, compete, and produce evidence. In 1998, he joined the St. Louis Rams as a backup. When starting quarterback Trent Green suffered a season-ending injury in 1999, Warner received the opportunity he had spent years preparing for. He threw 41 touchdown passes, won league MVP, led the Rams to a Super Bowl championship, and was named Super Bowl MVP. Warner later endured another period of doubt and career disruption before leading the Arizona Cardinals to their first Super Bowl appearance. His story is not just about waiting. It is about treating every less-visible level as a place where preparation still counts.",
