    "canonicalStory": "When Jackie Robinson joined the Brooklyn Dodgers in 1947, he broke Major League Baseball's modern color barrier. The achievement is often summarized as a historic first. That description can hide the daily cost. Robinson faced racist insults, threats, discriminatory treatment, hostile crowds, and opposition from within the sport. Branch Rickey selected him not only for athletic ability but because the assignment required emotional control under deliberate provocation. Robinson was expected to compete at the highest level while absorbing treatment no athlete should have been asked to endure. He did more than remain on the roster. Robinson won the inaugural Rookie of the Year Award, later became National League MVP, and helped the Dodgers win a World Series. His speed, intelligence, defense, and competitive force made it impossible to portray integration as charity. He belonged because he was excellent. Robinson's restraint should not be misread as passivity. It was strategic courage. He accepted a specific burden to open a door, then used his voice as a civil-rights advocate throughout and after his career. This story requires historical honesty. The inspiration is not that racism made him stronger. Racism was wrong. The inspiration lies in Robinson's courage, skill, sacrifice, and impact despite it---and in the responsibility his story places on institutions and teammates to challenge injustice rather than merely admire the person enduring it.",
    "reelspiration": "Sometimes courage is not simply breaking a barrier. It is carrying the pressure long enough that the barrier can never be rebuilt.",
    "principle": "Personal excellence can expose unjust systems, but lasting change also requires allies and institutional action.",
    "nextStep": "Identify one barrier affecting someone around you. Take one action that reduces their burden instead of merely praising their resilience.",
    "whyItMatters": "Robinson's major-league career helped break baseball's color barrier and became part of the broader struggle against segregation. His endurance mattered far beyond sport because it forced institutions and audiences to confront exclusion in public view.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "In 1947, Jackie Robinson entered Major League Baseball knowing\nopponents, crowds, and even people inside the sport would try to drive\nhim out. He faced racist abuse, threats, and pressure no athlete should\nhave carried. But Robinson was not placed in the lineup as a symbol\nalone. He performed. He won Rookie of the Year, later became National\nLeague MVP, and helped the Dodgers win a World Series. His restraint was\nnot weakness. It was strategic courage used to keep a door open for\nthose who would follow. Here is today's Reelspiration: Sometimes courage\nis not simply breaking a barrier. It is carrying the pressure long\nenough that the barrier can never be rebuilt.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He was competing against more than the other team.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- In 1947, Jackie Robinson broke Major League Baseball's\nmodern color barrier.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He faced racist abuse, threats, and deliberate provocation.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He had to control his response while performing at the\nhighest level.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He won Rookie of the Year and later National League MVP.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- His excellence helped make segregation in the sport\nindefensible.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Carry courage far enough that the\nbarrier cannot return.",
