    "canonicalStory": "In August 1976, Formula One champion Niki Lauda crashed at Germany's Nürburgring. His car caught fire, and fellow drivers helped pull him from the wreckage. Lauda suffered severe burns and lung damage from inhaling toxic fumes. A priest was reportedly called to his hospital bedside. Only six weeks later, Lauda returned to competition at the Italian Grand Prix. He finished fourth while still visibly injured. The comeback became one of motorsport's defining examples of physical courage. But Lauda's season contained a second decision that matters just as much. At the final race in Japan, heavy rain created dangerous conditions. After starting, Lauda withdrew because he believed the risk was unacceptable. The championship was at stake, and his rival James Hunt ultimately won the title by one point. Those two moments belong together. Returning after the crash demonstrated courage. Refusing to let pride dictate risk in Japan demonstrated judgment. Lauda returned to win additional world championships, proving that one lost title did not define his career. Reelspiration should resist the shallow lesson that bravery means ignoring fear. Lauda's story is stronger: fear can be acknowledged, risk can be evaluated, and a competitor can choose both audacity and restraint without surrendering identity.",
    "reelspiration": "Courage is not the absence of fear. It is knowing when to push through it---and when wisdom must have the final word.",
    "principle": "True courage combines commitment with honest risk assessment.",
    "nextStep": "Consider one risk you face. Separate what is genuinely necessary from what is only being demanded by pride.",
    "whyItMatters": "Lauda's return became one of motorsport's defining recovery stories, but his later decision to withdraw from the dangerous Japanese Grand Prix was equally important. Together, those choices showed that courage includes both returning and knowing when risk is no longer justified.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "In 1976, Formula One champion Niki Lauda crashed at the Nürburgring. His\ncar caught fire. He suffered severe burns and lung damage, and his life\nwas in danger. Six weeks later, he returned to racing and finished\nfourth at the Italian Grand Prix. But later that season, in dangerous\nrain at the championship finale, Lauda made another courageous decision:\nhe withdrew because he believed the conditions were not worth the risk.\nHe lost the title by one point---then returned to win championships\nagain. Here is today's Reelspiration: Courage is not the absence of\nfear. It is knowing when to push through it---and when wisdom must have\nthe final word.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- A crash nearly ended his life.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Niki Lauda suffered severe burns and lung damage at the\nNürburgring in 1976.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Six weeks later, he returned to Formula One competition.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- At the season finale, dangerous rain created another test.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- Lauda withdrew rather than let pride overrule his judgment.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He lost that title---then returned to become world champion\nagain.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Courage includes both the return and\nthe boundary.",
