    "canonicalStory": "One of sports' most repeated stories says Michael Jordan was cut from his high-school basketball team. The true version is more precise. As a sophomore at Laney High School, Jordan did not make the varsity roster and played junior varsity instead. He was still growing, still developing, and deeply disappointed. The distinction does not weaken the story. It improves it. Jordan was not rejected from the sport forever. He received evidence that, at that moment, he was not yet where he wanted to be. He responded by training, competing, and improving rather than treating the decision as a permanent verdict. Jordan became a varsity standout, earned a scholarship to the University of North Carolina, hit the championship-winning shot as a freshman in the 1982 NCAA title game, and entered the NBA. His professional résumé---six championships, five league MVP awards, and an unmatched cultural impact---made the high-school setback feel impossible in hindsight. But hindsight is exactly what young athletes do not have. At the time, Jordan could not see the championships. He could only see the roster he had not made. The useful lesson is not that every junior-varsity player becomes a global icon. It is that temporary placement is not permanent identity. Jordan's story is most powerful when stripped of exaggeration. He did not need a fictional rejection count or a false version of events. The real moment was enough: he was disappointed, and he converted the disappointment into disciplined development.",
    "reelspiration": "A temporary position is not a permanent identity. Let the setback show you what to build---not tell you who you are.",
    "principle": "Feedback becomes fuel when it is interpreted as information about development rather than a verdict on identity.",
    "nextStep": "Take one recent rejection or disappointing result. Write the specific skill gap it revealed and one practice that addresses it.",
    "whyItMatters": "Jordan's response to a junior-varsity placement became part of basketball's most influential development story. His career reshaped sport, marketing, global basketball culture, and the expectations attached to competitive excellence---without requiring the inaccurate claim that he was cut from basketball entirely.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "People often say Michael Jordan was cut from his high-school basketball\nteam. The more accurate story is that, as a sophomore, he did not make\nvarsity and played junior varsity instead. He was disappointed. He also\nreceived something useful: a clear picture of the gap between where he\nwas and where he wanted to be. Jordan trained, grew, and returned as a\nvarsity standout. He later won an NCAA championship, six NBA titles, and\nfive league MVP awards. Here is today's Reelspiration: A temporary\nposition is not a permanent identity. Let the setback show you what to\nbuild---not tell you who you are.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- The famous story is often told incorrectly.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Michael Jordan was not banished from basketball---he played\njunior varsity as a sophomore.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He was still deeply disappointed that he did not make\nvarsity.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- The decision showed him the gap between his goal and his\ncurrent level.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He responded by training, growing, and competing.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- That junior-varsity player later won six NBA championships.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Your current position is not your\npermanent identity.",
