    "canonicalStory": "Maria Skłodowska grew up in Warsaw when Poland was under Russian control and advanced education was restricted, especially for women. She studied through underground educational networks, worked as a governess, and saved money while helping support her sister's education. Only later could she travel to Paris and enroll at the Sorbonne. In Paris, she studied under difficult financial conditions and entered a scientific culture that offered women little room. Her investigation of uranium radiation led her and Pierre Curie to identify polonium and radium. The work was physically demanding, methodical, and performed with equipment far less sophisticated than the instruments scientists use today. In 1903, she shared the Nobel Prize in Physics. Three years later, Pierre died suddenly. Curie continued their work, took his university position, and became the first woman to teach at the Sorbonne. In 1911, she received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry---becoming the first person awarded Nobel Prizes in two scientific fields. During World War I, she helped organize mobile X-ray units so wounded soldiers could be examined closer to the front. Her story is not simply about brilliance. It is about gaining access, enduring loss, continuing the work, and using discovery in service of others.",
    "decision": "She decided that exclusion and grief would not end the investigation.",
    "reelspiration": "A closed door can delay your work. It does not get to decide the value of what you may discover.",
    "principle": "Access matters, but determined learning can preserve momentum until opportunity arrives.",
    "nextStep": "Identify one barrier between you and the knowledge you need. Find one alternate route into that knowledge today.",
    "whyItMatters": "Without Curie's persistence, the early science of radioactivity, the discovery and isolation of new elements, and wartime mobile X-ray work would have developed differently---and likely more slowly.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Marie Curie could not attend a traditional university in\nRussian-controlled Poland. So she studied through underground classes,\nworked to support her family, and saved enough to continue her education\nin Paris. There, in difficult conditions and in a scientific world that\nrarely welcomed women, she pursued questions about radiation. Her work\nhelped reveal polonium and radium. Then her husband and research\npartner, Pierre, died suddenly. Curie continued. She became the first\nwoman to teach at the Sorbonne and the first person to win two Nobel\nPrizes in different sciences. Here is today's Reelspiration: A closed\ndoor can delay your work. It does not get to decide the value of what\nyou may discover.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- The university door was closed to women like her.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Marie Curie studied through underground education in\nRussian-controlled Poland.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- She worked and saved until she could continue her studies in\nParis.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Her research helped reveal polonium and radium.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- After Pierre Curie died, she continued the work they had\nbegun.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- She became the first person awarded two Nobel Prizes in\ndifferent sciences.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- A closed door can delay your work. It\ncannot define its value.",
