    "canonicalStory": "Katherine Johnson's talent with numbers was visible early. Because her West Virginia hometown did not offer public schooling for Black children beyond eighth grade, her family moved during the school year so the children could continue their education. Johnson entered high school at thirteen and graduated from college at eighteen. In 1953, she joined the segregated West Area Computing unit at the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics, NASA's predecessor. She asked questions, requested access to meetings where women had not traditionally participated, and moved from routine calculation into flight research and orbital mechanics. Johnson calculated the trajectory for Alan Shepard's 1961 spaceflight. In 1962, as NASA prepared John Glenn's orbital mission, Glenn asked for Johnson to personally verify the electronic computer's calculations. Her work also supported Apollo missions, the Space Shuttle, and satellite programs. The technology was historic, but so was the trust she earned through precision. Johnson did not wait for the institution to become fair before doing work worthy of history. She mastered the problem, insisted on contributing, and left the institution changed by the quality of what she delivered.",
    "decision": "She decided to ask for access to the work her ability had prepared her to do.",
    "reelspiration": "When the system underestimates you, make the quality of your work impossible to ignore.",
