    "canonicalStory": "Louis Braille lost his sight after a childhood accident in his father's workshop and a resulting infection. His parents refused to assume blindness meant dependence. They taught him to navigate his village and pursued an education that eventually took him to the Royal Institute for Blind Youth in Paris. The school's raised-print books were large, expensive, and difficult to read. Students could feel the shapes of letters, but writing independently was far harder. Braille encountered a military system of raised dots intended for communication in darkness and recognized that a smaller, simpler code could become something more. As a teenager, he developed a six-dot cell that allowed letters, punctuation, numbers, and later music to be read through touch and written efficiently. The system was not immediately adopted by authorities. Braille continued teaching and refining it while recognition came slowly. He died before seeing his code achieve worldwide acceptance. Yet his design endured because it solved the problem from the user's point of view. Braille did not merely adapt to a world built for sight. He redesigned access to written language.",
    "decision": "He decided not merely to use the available system, but to build a better one.",
    "reelspiration": "Sometimes the obstacle that limits your access gives you the clearest view of what must be redesigned.",
    "principle": "Lived experience can reveal design failures that outsiders overlook.",
    "nextStep": "Write down one recurring frustration you understand firsthand. Ask what a more accessible design would look like.",
    "whyItMatters": "Braille's code transformed literacy, education, music, employment, and independent communication for blind people around the world.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Louis Braille lost his sight as a child. At school in Paris, he could\nread raised letters, but the books were enormous and writing\nindependently remained difficult. Then he encountered a military code\nmade from raised dots. At only fifteen, Braille began transforming the\nidea into a compact six-dot system that blind readers could read and\nwrite by touch. Authorities did not immediately embrace it, and he died\nbefore seeing its worldwide adoption. But the system carried his name\nacross languages and generations. Here is today's Reelspiration:\nSometimes the obstacle that limits your access gives you the clearest\nview of what must be redesigned.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He lost his sight---but not his hunger to read.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Raised-print books were slow, bulky, and difficult to\nproduce.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- A military dot code gave Louis Braille the seed of an idea.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- At fifteen, he developed a compact six-dot reading system.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- The system allowed blind people to read and write\nindependently.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Recognition came slowly, but the design endured worldwide.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Your obstacle may reveal what the\nworld needs redesigned.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Child's hand near leatherworking tools---no graphic injury.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Finger tracing oversized raised letters.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Night-writing dot grid transforming into six-dot cells.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Teenage hands punching dots with a slate and stylus.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Libraries, music notation, and modern refreshable braille\ndisplays.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card embossed in both print and braille.",
