    "canonicalStory": "Percy Julian grew up in Alabama in an era when educational opportunity for Black students was deliberately restricted. He entered DePauw University as a sub-freshman because his prior schooling had not provided the preparation offered to white classmates. He filled those gaps while completing college-level work and graduated as valedictorian. Even with outstanding credentials, racism limited academic appointments and research opportunities. Julian continued training, earned a doctorate in Vienna, and returned to tackle complex chemical synthesis. In 1935, he and Josef Pikl achieved the first total synthesis of physostigmine, a drug used in the treatment of glaucoma. Industrial laboratories also resisted hiring Black chemists. Julian eventually joined the Glidden Company, where his work with soybeans contributed to large-scale production of useful compounds and more affordable steroid intermediates. He later founded his own laboratory and became a successful entrepreneur. Julian's story is not evidence that discrimination can be defeated by excellence alone. The discrimination was real and costly. His achievement shows what becomes possible when ability is given room---and how much society loses when prejudice keeps talent outside the laboratory.",
