    "canonicalStory": "Grace Hopper earned a doctorate in mathematics and joined the U.S. Navy Reserve during World War II. Assigned to the Harvard Mark I project, she entered computing when programming meant working close to the machine's hardware and numerical instructions. Hopper became convinced that computers should translate more human-readable instructions into machine code. The concept challenged assumptions about what programming had to look like. She helped develop early compiler technology and advocated for machine-independent programming languages, work that influenced the development of COBOL. Her career also resisted conventional timelines. After retiring from the Navy Reserve at sixty, she was recalled to active duty because her expertise was still needed. She continued serving until age seventy-nine, becoming the oldest commissioned officer in the Navy at retirement. Hopper's gift was not only technical. She translated between people and machines, between ideas and institutions, and between what existed and what could become easier. She kept asking why complexity had to remain a permanent cost of participation.",
    "decision": "She decided that \"this is how computers work\" was not a sufficient reason to preserve needless difficulty.",
    "reelspiration": "The smartest innovation may not make the machine more powerful. It may make the power accessible to more people.",
    "principle": "Reducing unnecessary complexity can unlock participation at a massive scale.",
    "nextStep": "Find one process that forces people to learn the system's language. Redesign one step so the system better understands the person.",
    "whyItMatters": "Compiler technology and standardized programming languages helped move computing from specialized machinery toward a broadly usable platform for government, business, and science.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Early computers demanded instructions written close to the language of\nthe machine. Grace Hopper believed people should be able to write\ncommands in language closer to the way humans communicate. Many experts\nconsidered that impractical. Hopper kept developing compiler\ntechnology---the tools that translate human-readable instructions into\nmachine code---and helped advance programming languages that opened\ncomputing to far more people. Her career kept defying timelines too:\nafter retiring from the Navy at sixty, she was recalled and served until\nseventy-nine. Here is today's Reelspiration: The smartest innovation may\nnot make the machine more powerful. It may make the power accessible to\nmore people.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- Programming once required people to think like the machine.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Grace Hopper believed machines should do more of the\ntranslating.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Experts doubted that English-like programming could work.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- She helped pioneer compiler technology.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- Her work influenced languages that expanded access to\ncomputing.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- At 60, the Navy recalled her; she served until age 79.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Great innovation often makes power\neasier to use.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Mark I relays, paper tape, and handwritten numeric\ninstructions.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Hopper drawing a bridge between human words and machine\ncode.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Compiler animation translating commands.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Navy service timeline with retirement and recall.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Modern software environments branching from early language\nwork.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Make the power easier to use.\"",
