    "canonicalStory": "Hedy Lamarr became one of Hollywood's most recognizable stars, but fame created a narrow frame around how people understood her. Away from film sets, she experimented, sketched, and pursued technical problems. During World War II, Lamarr worked with composer George Antheil on a frequency-hopping communication system intended to make radio-controlled torpedoes harder to detect or jam. They received a U.S. patent in 1942. The Navy did not adopt the design in its original wartime form, and neither inventor profited from it. Decades later, frequency-hopping and spread-spectrum ideas became recognized as important developments in secure wireless communication. Lamarr and Antheil received belated honors, including recognition from the Electronic Frontier Foundation; Lamarr was later inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame. The lesson is not that celebrity hid a secret hobby. It is that people often mistake the role they can see for the full range of a person's ability. Lamarr kept inventing even when the world had already decided what kind of value it expected from her.",
    "decision": "She decided to keep inventing even though the world rewarded her for something else.",
