    "canonicalStory": "George Washington Carver was born enslaved near the end of the Civil War and separated from his mother in infancy after they were kidnapped. As a child with fragile health and deep curiosity about plants, he became known locally as the \"Plant Doctor.\" Education required movement and persistence. Carver left home young, worked to support himself, and traveled in search of schools that admitted Black students. Highland College accepted him by correspondence but withdrew admission when he arrived and administrators saw that he was Black. Carver continued. He studied art at Simpson College, where a teacher recognized his botanical talent and encouraged him toward agricultural science. He became Iowa State's first Black student, earned bachelor's and master's degrees, and later led agricultural work at Tuskegee Institute for decades. His most enduring contribution was practical service: teaching crop rotation, soil restoration, nutrition, and alternative crops to farmers whose land and finances had been damaged by cotton dependence. Carver turned scientific knowledge into tools people could use.",
