    "canonicalStory": "Temple Grandin did not speak until early childhood and received intensive teaching and speech therapy. As a teenager, she endured bullying and struggled in conventional social environments. A science teacher and experiences on her aunt's ranch helped connect her visual thinking to real problems she wanted to solve. When Grandin entered the livestock industry in the 1970s, she encountered resistance as a woman in a field dominated by men. She learned to make her work visible through drawings, photographs, measurements, trade articles, and designs that could be evaluated on their results. Grandin used her ability to think in detailed pictures to understand how animals experience shadows, noise, movement, and facility layouts. She designed handling systems intended to reduce fear and improve animal welfare, and became a professor, author, consultant, and advocate for autistic and other neurodivergent people. Her story should not suggest every autistic person has the same gifts or path. Its power is more precise: difference can become valuable when education, mentorship, exposure, and opportunity help a person connect their way of thinking to a problem worth solving.",
