    "canonicalStory": "Garrett Morgan left school with limited formal education, moved north, hired a tutor, and taught himself mechanical repair. Starting with sewing machines, he built businesses and used practical observation to identify problems he could solve. After learning about deadly smoke conditions faced by firefighters and workers, Morgan developed a safety hood designed to draw cleaner air from near the ground. In 1916, after an explosion trapped workers in a gas-filled tunnel beneath Lake Erie, Morgan and other volunteers used safety hoods during rescue efforts. The demonstration proved the device's value, but racism complicated sales. Morgan sometimes used white representatives in demonstrations so potential buyers would evaluate the product before rejecting its inventor. He later patented a three-position traffic signal that introduced an all-stop interval between movement directions. Morgan's story reveals two kinds of problem solving: designing the invention and navigating a market distorted by prejudice. His work made public spaces safer while exposing the unnecessary barriers placed between useful ideas and the people who need them.",
    "decision": "He decided to keep building and demonstrating useful inventions even when buyers judged the inventor before the product.",
    "reelspiration": "Solving the problem is only part of innovation. Sometimes courage is continuing until the world can no longer ignore that the solution works.",
    "principle": "Practical observation plus persistent testing can turn everyday frustration into public benefit.",
    "nextStep": "Observe one avoidable danger or frustration today. Write a one-sentence design requirement for a safer solution.",
    "whyItMatters": "Morgan's inventions contributed to the development of respiratory safety equipment and modern traffic-control systems.",
