    "canonicalStory": "Stephen Hawking arrived at Cambridge to study cosmology while still uncertain about the direction of his work. In his early twenties, after falls and increasing physical difficulty, he was diagnosed with motor neurone disease. The diagnosis disrupted his assumptions about time and possibility. Hawking continued his research as the disease progressively limited movement and speech. With colleagues, students, caregivers, family, wheelchairs, and communication technology, he built a working life around changing physical realities. Adaptation was not a single triumphant event; it was an ongoing infrastructure of support. His scientific work connected gravity, quantum theory, and thermodynamics, leading to the prediction that black holes emit radiation---now known as Hawking radiation. He also brought complex cosmology to a broad public through A Brief History of Time and decades of lectures and media appearances. The responsible lesson is not that illness can be defeated through optimism. Hawking's disease remained real. The inspiration lies in refusing to equate physical dependence with intellectual insignificance---and in recognizing the people and technology that made participation possible.",
