    "canonicalStory": "Abraham Lincoln did not move through public life in a straight line. He lost important elections, endured periods of profound melancholy, and spent years building a reputation through law, debate, and public argument. His defeat in the 1858 Senate race did not end his political future; the debates helped make his ideas known nationally. When Lincoln became president in 1861, seven Southern states had already declared secession. The crisis expanded into civil war. Every decision carried human cost. He replaced generals, faced criticism from nearly every direction, and tried to preserve constitutional government while the country fought over its survival and the future of slavery. Lincoln's position on slavery developed over time. He moved from opposing its expansion to issuing the Emancipation Proclamation and supporting the Thirteenth Amendment. That growth matters. Reelspiration should not flatten him into a flawless hero; it should show a leader learning under pressure and accepting that preserving the Union increasingly required confronting slavery directly. Lincoln did not live to guide Reconstruction. But his persistence through defeat, grief, war, and moral complexity helped preserve the United States and place abolition into the nation's constitutional future. His story reminds us that leadership is not proved by avoiding doubt. It is proved by continuing to think, decide, and grow when the stakes become larger than the leader.",
    "decision": "He decided to remain responsible for the whole nation, even when nearly every available choice carried loss.",
    "reelspiration": "A setback does not disqualify you from future leadership. Persistence matters most when it is joined by the willingness to grow.",
    "principle": "Resilience without reflection can repeat old errors; resilient leadership keeps learning under pressure.",
    "nextStep": "Revisit one belief or plan that recent evidence has challenged. Improve it instead of defending it automatically.",
    "whyItMatters": "Lincoln's wartime leadership preserved the Union, shifted the war toward emancipation, and helped create the conditions for the Thirteenth Amendment.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Abraham Lincoln lost elections, lived with deep melancholy, and entered\nthe presidency as the United States began to break apart. Civil war\nforced decisions with enormous human consequences. Lincoln changed\ngenerals, absorbed criticism, and kept the government functioning\nthrough national catastrophe. His thinking on slavery also changed. He\nmoved from opposing its expansion to issuing the Emancipation\nProclamation and supporting the amendment that abolished slavery.\nLincoln was not flawless, and that is part of the lesson. Here is\ntoday's Reelspiration: A setback does not disqualify you from future\nleadership. But leadership requires more than persistence---it requires\nthe willingness to grow when history proves your earlier understanding\nincomplete.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He lost major elections before becoming president.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- His Senate defeat helped carry his ideas to a national\naudience.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He entered office as the nation was breaking apart.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- War brought criticism, grief, and decisions with terrible\nconsequences.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- His public position on slavery expanded under the pressure\nof events and principle.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He helped preserve the Union and supported constitutional\nabolition.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Persistence matters most when it is\njoined by growth.",
