    "collections": [
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Born enslaved in Maryland, Harriet Tubman experienced family separation, forced labor, and a severe head injury after an overseer threw a heavy object that struck her. She lived with lasting effects from that injury. In 1849, facing the possibility of being sold, she escaped north. Reaching freedom could have been the end of the journey. Instead, Tubman returned to Maryland repeatedly to guide family members and others toward freedom through the Underground Railroad. The work depended on secrecy, local knowledge, trusted networks, and the courage of the freedom seekers themselves. It was not a solitary legend; it was organized resistance against slavery. During the Civil War, Tubman served the Union as a nurse, scout, spy, and guide. She helped lead the Combahee River Raid, which liberated hundreds of enslaved people. After the war, she continued caring for family and advocating for women's suffrage, even while struggling for fair compensation for her wartime service. Tubman's life was not simply a story of fearlessness. Courage does not require the absence of fear. Her achievement was choosing action despite danger---and repeatedly turning personal freedom into responsibility for others.",
