    "canonicalStory": "Winston Churchill's career included influence, controversy, serious errors, and long periods outside power. By the late 1930s, his warnings about Nazi Germany had made him seem isolated to many. In May 1940, as German forces swept through Western Europe, he became prime minister. Britain faced the evacuation at Dunkirk, the fall of France, and the possibility of invasion. Churchill's speeches did not deny the danger. They named it. He told Parliament and the public that the struggle could be prolonged and costly, while insisting that capitulation was not the only realistic response. His language helped sustain national morale through the Battle of Britain and the Blitz until Britain was joined by larger Allied power. But historical honesty matters: victory was not Churchill's alone. It depended on service members and civilians across the British Empire and Commonwealth, resistance movements, the Soviet Union's immense sacrifice, and the industrial and military strength of the United States and other Allies. Churchill's inspiring 1940 leadership must also be presented alongside his imperial views and policies, some of which caused or deepened suffering. Reelspiration can honor a consequential decision without turning a complicated person into an uncomplicated symbol.",
    "decision": "He decided Britain would continue resisting Nazi Germany rather than treat negotiation or surrender as unavoidable.",
    "reelspiration": "Hope is not pretending the danger is small. Sometimes hope is telling the truth---and still refusing to call defeat inevitable.",
    "principle": "Credible courage names reality clearly and preserves agency inside it.",
    "nextStep": "Describe your present challenge without exaggerating or minimizing it. Then name the next action that remains available.",
    "whyItMatters": "Churchill's 1940 leadership helped sustain British resistance at a moment when Nazi control of Europe appeared increasingly possible.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "In May 1940, much of Western Europe was collapsing under Nazi attack.\nBritain faced military disaster, evacuation, and the possibility of\ninvasion. Winston Churchill had spent years warning about the threat,\noften from the political margins. Now he had to lead. His speeches did\nnot promise an easy victory. They described the danger and made\ncontinued resistance feel possible. Britain held through the Battle of\nBritain and the Blitz until a much larger Allied effort defeated Nazi\nGermany. Here is today's Reelspiration: Hope is not pretending the\ndanger is small. Sometimes hope is telling the truth---and still\nrefusing to call defeat inevitable.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He spent years outside power warning about Nazi Germany.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- In 1940, he became prime minister during military collapse.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Britain faced evacuation, bombing, and possible invasion.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Churchill did not hide the danger from the public.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He used language to make continued resistance imaginable.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Victory required an enormous Allied effort far beyond one\nleader.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Tell the truth about the danger\nwithout surrendering the future.",
