    "canonicalStory": "Nelson Mandela joined the struggle against South Africa's apartheid system, which enforced white minority rule through law and violence. After peaceful resistance was met with repression, he helped establish an armed wing of the African National Congress. Arrested and convicted at the Rivonia Trial, he was sentenced to life imprisonment. Mandela spent twenty-seven years imprisoned, much of it on Robben Island. Prison did not remove the political conflict outside. It also did not turn him into a passive saint. He remained a strategist, studied his opponents, and became part of secret contacts that eventually opened negotiations. When released in 1990, Mandela did not abandon justice. He continued demanding democratic rule while negotiating with President F. W. de Klerk and confronting violence from multiple directions. In 1994, South Africa held its first fully democratic national election, and Mandela became president. Reconciliation did not solve inequality, erase the past, or remove controversy from Mandela's record. Its inspiration lies in a disciplined choice: he used power to widen participation rather than narrow it around vengeance. Forgiveness, in this context, was not forgetting. It was refusing to let the future be designed only by the injuries of the past.",
    "decision": "He decided to negotiate a democratic transition rather than use his authority to build a politics centered only on revenge.",
    "reelspiration": "Forgiveness does not erase injustice. At its strongest, it refuses to let injustice design the future.",
    "principle": "Reconciliation is meaningful only when joined to truth, power-sharing, and structural change.",
    "nextStep": "Separate accountability from retaliation in one conflict. Decide what repair would actually improve the future.",
    "whyItMatters": "Mandela's leadership helped guide South Africa from apartheid toward democratic elections while reducing the risk of wider civil war.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Nelson Mandela fought South Africa's apartheid system and was sentenced\nto life in prison. He spent twenty-seven years behind bars. When he was\nreleased, the country remained divided and violent. Mandela had reasons\nto seek revenge---but he also understood that replacing one form of\ndomination with another would not create democracy. He negotiated while\ncontinuing to demand majority rule. In 1994, South Africans voted in the\ncountry's first fully democratic national election, and Mandela became\npresident. Here is today's Reelspiration: Forgiveness does not erase\ninjustice. At its strongest, it refuses to let injustice design the\nfuture.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- Apartheid used law and violence to enforce white minority\nrule.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Nelson Mandela was sentenced to life imprisonment.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He spent twenty-seven years behind bars.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Prison did not end his political thinking or resolve.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- After release, he negotiated without abandoning democratic\ndemands.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- In 1994, South Africa held its first fully democratic\nnational election.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Do not let yesterday's injustice\ndesign tomorrow's system.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Apartheid signs and divided public spaces.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Robben Island cell and quarry---quiet, not sensational.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Mandela studying and speaking with fellow prisoners.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Release from prison and tense negotiation rooms.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Long voting lines in 1994.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Do not let injustice design the future.\"",
