    "collections": [
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Frederick Douglass was born enslaved in Maryland. As a boy in Baltimore, Sophia Auld began teaching him the alphabet until her husband stopped the lessons, arguing that literacy would make an enslaved person unfit for bondage. Douglass recognized the warning as a map: education was dangerous to slavery because it expanded thought and possibility. He continued learning through exchanges with white children, discarded newspapers, and persistent practice. Literacy did not immediately free him. In some ways it made captivity more painful because he could better understand the system around him. But it also equipped him to imagine, plan, and eventually escape. After reaching freedom, Douglass became an abolitionist speaker, writer, newspaper publisher, and advocate for women's rights and Black citizenship. His autobiographies forced readers to confront slavery through the testimony of someone the institution had tried to silence. Douglass's story demonstrates why access to knowledge is never neutral. Learning can reveal the structure of a cage before it provides the means to leave it. The decision to keep learning was not merely self-improvement. It was preparation for freedom and public service.",
    "decision": "He decided to continue learning after formal instruction was deliberately taken away.",
    "reelspiration": "When a system fears your education, learning becomes more than improvement. It becomes liberation.",
    "principle": "Knowledge expands agency by revealing both the structure of oppression and alternatives to it.",
    "nextStep": "Find one source of knowledge that increases your independence. Study it for twenty focused minutes today.",
    "whyItMatters": "Douglass's writing and oratory became central to abolition, Black citizenship, and democratic reform in the nineteenth-century United States.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Frederick Douglass was born enslaved in Maryland. When he began learning\nthe alphabet, the lessons were stopped. His enslaver argued that\nliteracy would make an enslaved person impossible to control. Douglass\nunderstood the message: education threatened the system holding him. So\nhe kept learning---through children, newspapers, and relentless\npractice. Literacy did not instantly free him, but it helped him\nunderstand slavery, imagine another life, and prepare for escape. After\nreaching freedom, he became one of America's most powerful abolitionist\nwriters and speakers. Here is today's Reelspiration: When a system fears\nyour education, learning becomes more than improvement. It becomes\nliberation.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He was told education would make him impossible to control.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Frederick Douglass understood why literacy was forbidden.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He continued learning through every route he could find.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Knowledge made slavery clearer---and freedom imaginable.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He escaped and became an abolitionist writer and speaker.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- The voice slavery tried to silence helped expose the\ninstitution.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Learn what the system benefits from\nyou not knowing.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Young Douglass with alphabet lesson interrupted.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Wharf and street exchanges used to continue learning.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Newspaper words becoming a map of the wider world.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Escape represented symbolically, without inventing\noperational details.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Lecture hall, printing press, and published narrative.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Learn what the system benefits from you not\nknowing.\"",
