    "collections": [
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "In 1914, Ernest Shackleton led the Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedition with the goal of crossing the continent. Before the crossing could begin, the ship Endurance became trapped in pack ice in the Weddell Sea. The ice eventually crushed and sank it. The original objective was over. Shackleton's leadership was tested by whether he could recognize that fact quickly enough. The expedition camped on drifting ice, launched small boats through dangerous seas, and reached remote Elephant Island. Shackleton then sailed with five companions roughly 800 miles across the Southern Ocean in the James Caird to seek rescue. After landing on South Georgia, Shackleton and two others crossed the island's unmapped interior to reach a whaling station. Multiple rescue attempts followed before the men remaining on Elephant Island were recovered. All members of the Endurance party survived, though the wider expedition included loss elsewhere. The story is not that Shackleton never failed. The crossing failed completely. His leadership became memorable because he changed the definition of success when reality changed. He preserved routine, watched morale, made hard logistical choices, and treated the lives of the crew as the mission that remained.",
    "decision": "He decided that completing the crossing no longer mattered; the survival and rescue of the expedition did.",
    "reelspiration": "When the original goal becomes impossible, leadership is knowing what must not be lost with it.",
    "principle": "Adaptive leaders separate the purpose from the plan and protect people when the plan collapses.",
    "nextStep": "Name one plan that reality has invalidated. Preserve the underlying purpose and define a new measure of success.",
    "whyItMatters": "The Endurance survival story became a foundational case study in crisis leadership, morale, adaptation, and expedition logistics.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Ernest Shackleton sailed toward Antarctica with an enormous goal: cross\nthe continent. Before the expedition could begin, the Endurance became\ntrapped in ice and was eventually crushed. The original mission was\ngone. Shackleton changed the objective. Success now meant bringing his\npeople home. The crew survived on drifting ice, crossed dangerous water\nin small boats, and reached Elephant Island. Shackleton then sailed\nroughly 800 miles through the Southern Ocean to find help. Every member\nof the Endurance party was rescued. Here is today's Reelspiration: When\nthe original goal becomes impossible, leadership is knowing what must\nnot be lost with it.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- The expedition set out to cross Antarctica.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- The Endurance became trapped and was crushed by ice.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- The original mission became impossible.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Shackleton redefined success: keep the crew alive.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- The men crossed ice and open ocean to reach Elephant Island.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- A small rescue voyage crossed roughly 800 miles of deadly\nsea.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Change the objective before you\nsacrifice what matters most.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Endurance entering Antarctic pack ice.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Hull compressed and crew unloading supplies.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Camp routine on drifting ice.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Small boats reaching Elephant Island.\n\nSCENE 5 --- James Caird in massive seas and South Georgia crossing.\n\nSCENE 6 --- Rescue reunion and end card: \"Protect what matters when the\nplan breaks.\"",
