    "canonicalStory": "Desmond Doss was a Seventh-day Adventist who believed he could serve his country as a medic but could not carry a weapon or kill. The Army classified him as a conscientious objector, a label he disliked because he did not object to serving. His position brought ridicule, pressure, and conflict during training. Doss remained with his unit and proved himself as a medic in the Pacific. During fighting on Okinawa in 1945, American forces withdrew from the Maeda Escarpment while wounded soldiers remained above. Doss stayed and repeatedly treated men and lowered them down the cliff to safety. Official accounts credit him with saving approximately seventy-five soldiers during that action, though Doss himself estimated fewer and others estimated more. He was later wounded and continued prioritizing other injured men. President Harry Truman awarded him the Medal of Honor---the first conscientious objector to receive it. The power of Doss's story is not that everyone should make the same moral choice. It is that courage and conviction did not have to be opposites. He found a form of service consistent with his beliefs and then accepted extraordinary personal risk to fulfill it.",
    "decision": "He decided to remain on the battlefield as a medic after others withdrew, returning for one wounded person after another.",
    "reelspiration": "Courage is not abandoning your convictions when pressure rises. It is finding the hardest honorable way to live them.",
    "principle": "Integrity becomes credible when it is paired with service and personal responsibility.",
    "nextStep": "Name one conviction you want to preserve. Identify the demanding form of service that proves it is more than a slogan.",
    "whyItMatters": "Doss became the first conscientious objector awarded the Medal of Honor and a lasting example of noncombatant courage in military service.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Desmond Doss wanted to serve in World War II, but his faith would not\nallow him to carry a weapon or take a life. He trained as a medic and\nfaced ridicule from men who doubted he could be useful in combat. On\nOkinawa, when his unit withdrew from the Maeda Escarpment, wounded\nsoldiers remained behind. Doss stayed. Under fire, he treated men and\nlowered them down the cliff one after another---officially credited with\nsaving about seventy-five. He later received the Medal of Honor. Here is\ntoday's Reelspiration: Courage is not abandoning your convictions when\npressure rises. It is finding the hardest honorable way to live them.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He volunteered to serve but refused to carry a weapon.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Desmond Doss believed his duty was to save life, not take\nit.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- His conviction brought ridicule and pressure during\ntraining.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- At Okinawa, wounded soldiers were left above the escarpment.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- Doss stayed under fire and lowered men to safety.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He was officially credited with saving about seventy-five\nsoldiers.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Find the hardest honorable way to live\nyour convictions.",
