    "canonicalStory": "Ulysses S. Grant graduated from West Point and served with distinction in the Mexican-American War, but his first military career ended unhappily. He resigned from the Army in 1854. In civilian life, farming and business efforts failed to provide reliable security. At one point he worked in his family's leather-goods store in Galena, Illinois. The Civil War reopened a door. Grant did not return as the nation's obvious future commander. He began by helping organize Illinois volunteers and gradually earned responsibility through results. At Forts Henry and Donelson, Vicksburg, and Chattanooga, he demonstrated an ability to coordinate movement, sustain pressure, and recover from setbacks. Grant's campaigns were costly, and historians continue to debate aspects of his generalship. Yet the old caricature that he succeeded only through reckless numbers is incomplete. He understood logistics, joint operations, maneuver, and the strategic reality that Confederate armies---not merely cities---had to be defeated. In 1864 he coordinated simultaneous pressure across multiple theaters. Grant also accepted Robert E. Lee's surrender at Appomattox with terms designed to reduce humiliation and help soldiers return home. His rise does not erase his failures, controversies, or later presidential difficulties. It demonstrates something more useful: a life can contain a period of drift and still produce a season of extraordinary responsibility.",
    "decision": "He accepted a modest reentry point and let consistent performance rebuild trust.",
    "reelspiration": "Your lowest season may be a chapter of preparation---not the final judgment on your usefulness.",
    "principle": "Second chances become meaningful when experience, humility, and readiness meet responsibility.",
    "nextStep": "Take the next available responsibility seriously, even if it feels smaller than the role you once imagined.",
    "whyItMatters": "Grant's campaigns helped preserve the Union and end the Civil War; his surrender terms at Appomattox influenced the transition from battlefield defeat toward reunion.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Before Ulysses S. Grant became the Union's leading general, he had\nresigned from the Army and struggled in civilian life. Farming and\nbusiness did not bring security. He worked in his family's store while\nthe promise of his early career seemed far behind him. Then the Civil\nWar created another chance to serve. Grant started by organizing\nvolunteers and earned larger commands through results. Fort Donelson.\nVicksburg. Chattanooga. Finally, command of all Union armies. His story\nis not that failure magically becomes success. It is that difficult\nyears do not erase your ability to answer when responsibility returns.\nHere is today's Reelspiration: Your lowest season may be a chapter of\npreparation---not the final judgment on your usefulness.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He left the Army and struggled to support his family.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Civilian ventures did not become the life he expected.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- The Civil War created a second chance to serve.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Grant earned responsibility through results, not reputation.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He learned to coordinate pressure across an entire war.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- At Appomattox, victory was paired with restraint.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- A difficult chapter does not cancel\nyour future usefulness.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Grant working quietly in a leather-goods store.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Volunteer regiments forming in Illinois.\n\nSCENE 3 --- River maps and Fort Donelson surrender correspondence.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Vicksburg trenches and logistics routes.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Appomattox meeting rendered with restraint.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"A difficult chapter does not cancel your\nusefulness.\"",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Avoid romanticizing war losses or portraying Grant as beyond criticism.\nDo not repeat unsupported claims about alcoholism as simple explanation\nfor every setback.",
    "sourceId": "RS-MC002",
