    "slug": "dwight-d-eisenhower",
    "subject": "Dwight D. Eisenhower",
    "dek": "He Led the Largest Allied Invasion in Europe---and Prepared to Take the Blame Alone",
    "challenges": [
      "need-leadership"
    ],
    "collections": [
      "military-courage",
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Dwight D. Eisenhower spent much of his early career in staff and training assignments rather than celebrated battlefield command. By 1942, however, his organizational ability and political judgment placed him in charge of increasingly complex Allied operations. His task was not simply to direct armies. It was to hold together a coalition of nations, commanders, services, personalities, and competing priorities. Planning the Normandy invasion required decisions about weather, deception, logistics, air power, naval support, airborne drops, and the lives of hundreds of thousands of service members. Forecasts were uncertain. A delay carried risks; launching carried others. Eisenhower listened to specialists and commanders, then made the final decision to go on June 6, 1944. He also drafted a short note in case the landings failed. In it, he accepted responsibility and did not blame the troops. That unused message became one of history's clearest examples of leadership accountability. Eisenhower understood that authority cannot claim success while outsourcing failure. D-Day did not end the war, and victory belonged to millions of Allied service members, resistance networks, industrial workers, and civilians. Eisenhower's contribution was the disciplined coordination of collective strength. His story reminds leaders that the highest form of command is not personal glory. It is creating conditions in which many people can succeed---and standing ready to own the consequences if they do not.",
    "decision": "He decided to launch the invasion and personally own the result.",
    "reelspiration": "Great leaders accept responsibility before they know the outcome.",
    "principle": "Accountability is most credible when it is chosen before success is certain.",
    "nextStep": "Before your next major decision, write down what responsibility you will accept if the outcome goes wrong.",
    "whyItMatters": "Eisenhower's coalition leadership helped make the Normandy invasion possible and accelerated the liberation of Western Europe.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Before D-Day, Dwight Eisenhower faced a decision that could alter the\nwar. Weather forecasts were uncertain. Delay was dangerous. Launching\nthe invasion meant placing thousands of lives at risk. Eisenhower\nlistened to commanders, forecasters, and planners. Then he made the\ndecision: go. But he also wrote a note in case the invasion failed. He\naccepted responsibility and did not blame the troops. The note was never\nneeded, but it revealed the leader behind the operation. Here is today's\nReelspiration: Authority is not the right to receive credit. It is the\nobligation to accept responsibility when the outcome is uncertain.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- D-Day required the coordination of nations, armies, ships,\naircraft, and intelligence.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- The weather forecast was uncertain.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Delay carried danger. Launching carried enormous risk.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Eisenhower listened---then made the final decision.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He wrote a note accepting responsibility if the invasion\nfailed.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He was prepared to carry blame rather than pass it downward.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Great leaders accept responsibility\nbefore they know the outcome.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Allied planning room filled with maps and weather charts.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Conflicting forecasts and commanders in discussion.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Eisenhower speaking with paratroopers before departure.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Handwritten failure note in close-up.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Landing craft approaching Normandy.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Accept responsibility before you know the\noutcome.\"",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Do not imply Eisenhower alone planned or won D-Day. Credit Allied\nplanners, commanders, troops, resistance members, and civilians. Avoid\ngraphic combat imagery.",
    "sourceId": "RS-MC003",
    "pattern": "Immense stakes • Coalition conflict • Uncertainty • Accountability",
