    "collections": [
      "military-courage",
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Horatio Nelson joined the Royal Navy as a boy and rose through experience, patronage, courage, and unusual tactical instinct. His career included setbacks as well as success. He lost sight in his right eye after the siege of Calvi and later lost his right arm after a failed attack at Santa Cruz de Tenerife. Nelson returned to service after both injuries. He cultivated close communication with captains and encouraged initiative within a shared plan. At the Battle of the Nile and later at Trafalgar, his approach emphasized aggressive concentration and breaking conventional patterns rather than rigidly following a parallel line of battle. At Trafalgar in 1805, the British fleet defeated a combined French and Spanish fleet, preventing Napoleon from challenging British naval control in the same way. Nelson was killed during the battle. His death contributed to a heroic national image that can obscure the complexity of empire, war, and the sailors whose collective action made victory possible. The transferable lesson is not that injury should be ignored or that leaders must expose themselves recklessly. It is that altered circumstances do not automatically erase judgment, experience, or purpose. Nelson adapted his command identity after loss and continued to contribute through trust, preparation, and tactical clarity.",
    "decision": "He chose to rebuild his command around remaining strengths rather than define himself only by injury.",
    "reelspiration": "Loss can change your method without ending your contribution.",
    "principle": "Adaptation preserves purpose when circumstances permanently change.",
    "nextStep": "Name one capability that remains strong after your setback. Design your next contribution around it.",
    "whyItMatters": "Nelson's victories shaped British naval dominance during the Napoleonic era and influenced naval doctrine and leadership culture.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Horatio Nelson lost sight in one eye during combat. Later, after a\nfailed attack, he lost his right arm. Either injury could have ended an\neighteenth-century naval career. Nelson returned to command. He built\nunusually strong trust with his captains and gave them room to act\nwithin a shared plan. At Trafalgar, his fleet broke conventional\nformation and defeated a larger combined enemy fleet. Nelson was killed\nin the battle, and the victory belonged to thousands of sailors---not\none man alone. Here is today's Reelspiration: What you lose may change\nhow you lead. It does not automatically erase what you still know, what\nyou can still give, or what your experience can make possible.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He lost sight in one eye.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- A failed attack later cost him his right arm.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Nelson returned to command.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He built trust and initiative among his captains.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- His tactics challenged conventional naval formation.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- At Trafalgar, the fleet prevailed---but Nelson did not\nsurvive.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Loss can change your method without\nending your contribution.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Young naval officer on a crowded wooden deck.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Medical recovery shown without graphic detail.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Captains gathered around a shared battle plan.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Fleet formations changing at Trafalgar.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Signal flags and collective action across ships.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Loss can change your method without ending your\ncontribution.\"",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Avoid ableist \"overcame disability\" language and glorification of death.\nAcknowledge imperial context and the contributions of sailors throughout\nthe fleet.",
