    "collections": [
      "military-courage",
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Alexander became king of Macedon at about twenty years old after the assassination of his father, Philip II. He inherited a highly trained army and a planned campaign against the Persian Empire. Over the next decade, he led forces through Asia Minor, Egypt, Mesopotamia, Central Asia, and into the Indian subcontinent. Alexander often shared danger with his troops and used speed, combined arms, and decisive maneuver. Victories at Issus and Gaugamela broke the power of the Achaemenid Persian Empire. His campaigns also caused destruction, mass death, and forced political change. The spread of Greek language and cultural exchange across the Hellenistic world emerged through conquest, not peaceful exploration. As the army moved farther east, exhaustion and resistance increased. At the Hyphasis River, his soldiers refused to continue deeper into India. Alexander eventually turned back. He died in Babylon in 323 BCE without a clear, stable succession plan. His empire fragmented among his generals. Alexander's story contains both inspiration and warning. Personal courage can create loyalty. Vision can redraw what people believe possible. But a leader who cannot define \"enough\" may build something too dependent on personal momentum to survive their absence. The true test of leadership is not only how far the leader can go, but what remains when the leader is gone.",
    "decision": "The missing decision was to convert conquest into stable succession before time ran out.",
    "reelspiration": "Leadership is not only how far you can push while you are present. It is what can continue after your momentum is gone.",
    "principle": "Durable leadership requires succession, institutions, and limits---not only personal brilliance.",
    "nextStep": "Document one critical process or develop one successor so the mission does not depend entirely on you.",
    "whyItMatters": "Alexander ended the Achaemenid Empire and helped create the Hellenistic world, but the fragmentation after his death demonstrates the fragility of leader-dependent systems.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Alexander became king at about twenty and spent the next decade\nconquering across three continents. He led from the front, moved\nrapidly, and defeated the Persian Empire in battles still studied today.\nHis courage inspired loyalty---but his ambition rarely accepted limits.\nEventually his soldiers refused to march farther into India. Alexander\ndied at thirty-two without a stable succession plan, and the empire\nfractured. Here is today's Reelspiration: Leadership is not only how far\nyou can push people while you are present. It is what can continue after\nyour momentum is gone.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He became king at about twenty years old.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- He inherited a powerful army and an enormous ambition.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Speed, combined arms, and personal courage brought repeated\nvictories.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- The campaigns also brought conquest, destruction, and\nexhaustion.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- At the Hyphasis, his soldiers refused to continue.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He died without a stable succession, and the empire\nfragmented.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Build something that can survive your\nabsence.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Young king taking command of Macedonian formations.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Maps of rapid campaigns across the Persian Empire.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Battle diagrams at Gaugamela.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Soldiers exhausted at the edge of India.\n\nSCENE 5 --- An empire map fragmenting after his death.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Build something that can survive your absence.\"",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Avoid glorifying conquest, invented speeches, and exact ancient numbers\npresented as certain. Acknowledge violence and reliance on Philip II's\nmilitary system.",
