    "slug": "admiral-yi-sun-sin",
    "subject": "Admiral Yi Sun-sin",
    "dek": "Removed, Imprisoned, and Recalled---He Returned When His Country Needed Him Most",
    "challenges": [
      "need-leadership"
    ],
    "collections": [
      "military-courage",
      "historic-decisions"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Admiral Yi Sun-sin served Korea's Joseon dynasty during the Japanese invasions of the 1590s. Through preparation, disciplined crews, coastal knowledge, and effective use of warships---including the famous turtle ships---Yi won a series of naval victories that disrupted Japanese supply lines. Court politics and false accusations led to his removal, imprisonment, torture, and reduction to the rank of common soldier. He was later restored to command only after the Korean fleet suffered catastrophic defeat under another commander. Yi reportedly returned to find just twelve serviceable ships, though additional vessels may have joined before battle. At the Battle of Myeongnyang in 1597, Yi used narrow waters, currents, positioning, and disciplined resistance against a much larger Japanese force. The victory preserved Korea's remaining naval capability. He continued serving until he was killed at the Battle of Noryang in 1598. Yi's story resonates because it joins competence with duty after injustice. He had reason for bitterness. Instead, when recalled, he focused on the national emergency. Reelspiration should not suggest that people must always return to institutions that harmed them. The lesson is that unfair treatment does not erase your ability---and that, when values and circumstances justify it, service can be larger than the insult.",
    "decision": "He accepted restored command and focused on the emergency rather than personal vindication.",
    "reelspiration": "Being pushed aside does not erase your ability. When the mission is larger than the insult, returning can become its own form of courage.",
    "principle": "Character is revealed when a person can separate justified hurt from the responsibility they still choose to carry.",
    "nextStep": "Decide whether your next move should protect your boundary, serve the larger mission, or do both. Choose deliberately---not from wounded pride alone.",
    "whyItMatters": "Yi's naval victories protected Korean supply routes and resistance during the Japanese invasions, and he remains one of Korea's most revered historical commanders.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Admiral Yi Sun-sin had already won major naval victories when court\npolitics turned against him. He was removed, imprisoned, tortured, and\nreduced in rank. Then Korea's fleet was nearly destroyed, and Yi was\nrecalled. He returned to command with only a small number of ships\nagainst a much larger Japanese force. At Myeongnyang, he used narrow\nwaters, currents, preparation, and disciplined crews to hold the line.\nHere is today's Reelspiration: Being pushed aside does not erase your\nability. And when the mission is larger than the insult, returning can\nbecome its own form of courage.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He prepared Korea's navy and disrupted enemy supply lines.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Political accusations led to removal, prison, and torture.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- After disaster, the court called him back.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He returned to a fleet reduced to a handful of ships.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- At Myeongnyang, terrain, currents, and discipline offset\noverwhelming numbers.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- He served until his death at Noryang.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Unfair treatment does not erase your\nability.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Prepared Korean fleet and coastal charts.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Court accusation, imprisonment, and stripped insignia shown\nsymbolically.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Yi returning to a nearly destroyed fleet.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Myeongnyang currents visualized through water and map\nanimation.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Small formation holding against a larger fleet.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card: \"Unfair treatment does not erase your ability.\"",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Treat Korean and Japanese history with care. Avoid uncertain exact fleet\ncounts as settled fact and avoid nationalist dehumanization. Do not\nimply returning to an abusive institution is always required.",
    "sourceId": "RS-MC010",
