    "slug": "audie-murphy",
    "subject": "Audie Murphy",
    "dek": "The Young Soldier Who Kept Protecting His Company After He Was Wounded",
    "challenges": [
      "need-courage"
    ],
    "collections": [
      "military-courage"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Audie Murphy grew up in rural Texas in severe poverty. After his mother died, he helped support younger siblings and tried to enlist following Pearl Harbor. The Marine Corps and Navy rejected him; the Army accepted him in 1942. He was small, young, and hardly the image of the warrior later created around him. In January 1945 near Holtzwihr, France, Murphy ordered his company back to a safer position while he stayed forward directing artillery. He climbed onto a burning tank destroyer and used its machine gun against advancing German forces, continuing after being wounded. He later reorganized his men for a counterattack. Murphy returned home celebrated, but the war did not end inside him. He spoke publicly about nightmares, anxiety, and dependence on sleeping medication at a time when combat trauma was rarely discussed honestly. His life warns us not to romanticize courage as invulnerability. He acted while afraid---and then carried consequences that medals could not erase.",
    "decision": "He decided the safety of his company mattered more than his own immediate escape.",
    "reelspiration": "Courage is not proof that fear disappeared. It is what someone protects while fear is still present.",
    "principle": "Real bravery includes both decisive action and honesty about the wounds that remain afterward.",
    "nextStep": "Protect one person or responsibility within your reach today---without pretending you have no fear.",
    "whyItMatters": "Murphy's battlefield actions protected his company, while his later openness helped expose the hidden psychological cost of combat.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Audie Murphy was small, young, and raised in poverty. The Marines and\nNavy rejected him. The Army accepted him. In France in 1945, German\nforces attacked his company. Murphy ordered his soldiers back while he\nstayed forward directing artillery. Wounded and exposed, he climbed onto\na burning tank destroyer and used its machine gun until the attack\nbroke. But the medals did not erase the war. Murphy later spoke about\nnightmares and combat trauma. Here is today's Reelspiration: Courage is\nnot proof that fear disappeared. It is what you choose to protect while\nfear is still present.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He was rejected by two service branches.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- The Army accepted the undersized teenager from Texas.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- In France, his company faced tanks and advancing infantry.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He sent his men back and remained forward.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- Wounded, he fought from a burning tank destroyer.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- After the war, he spoke about the wounds medals could not\nshow.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Courage protects something even while\nfear remains.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Dusty Texas childhood and family responsibility.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Enlistment desk and rejection stamps.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Snowy battlefield and soldiers withdrawing.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Burning tank destroyer shown without graphic violence.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Quiet postwar bedroom and sleepless silhouette.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card with the Reelspiration line.",
    "editorialGuardrails": "Do not glorify killing or reduce Murphy to a battlefield statistic.\nInclude his postwar trauma and avoid presenting war as a route to\npersonal greatness.",
    "sourceId": "RS-M001",
    "pattern": "Poverty • Youth • Combat • Postwar trauma",
