    "collections": [
      "military-courage"
    ],
    "canonicalStory": "Doris \"Dorie\" Miller enlisted in the U.S. Navy in 1939 as a mess attendant, one of the limited ratings generally available to Black sailors in the segregated service. On December 7, 1941, he was collecting laundry aboard USS West Virginia when the attack on Pearl Harbor began. His normal battle station had been destroyed. Miller helped move wounded sailors, including the ship's mortally wounded captain. He then manned a .50-caliber antiaircraft gun despite having no formal training on the weapon and fired until his ammunition was gone. Miller became the first Black recipient of the Navy Cross. Recognition did not immediately dismantle segregation, and he later died when USS Liscome Bay was torpedoed in 1943. His story is not that discrimination disappears during crisis. It is that ability can exist long before institutions are willing to recognize it.",
    "decision": "When the attack came, he moved toward the wounded and took responsibility for a task he had never been allowed to train for.",
    "reelspiration": "Your assigned position may describe where a system placed you. It does not define the full measure of what you can do.",
    "principle": "Capability often appears before permission, title, or recognition.",
    "nextStep": "Learn one skill beyond the limits of your current title---and be prepared to use it in service of others.",
    "whyItMatters": "Miller's heroism challenged stereotypes about Black sailors and became part of the pressure for broader opportunity and desegregation in the armed forces.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Doris Miller served in a segregated Navy that largely assigned Black\nsailors to service jobs. During the attack on Pearl Harbor, his battle\nstation was destroyed. He helped carry wounded sailors to safety, then\ntook control of an antiaircraft gun he had never been formally trained\nto use. Miller fired until the ammunition was gone. He became the first\nBlack sailor awarded the Navy Cross. Here is today's Reelspiration: Your\nassigned position may describe where a system placed you. It does not\ndefine the full measure of what you can do.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- The Navy assigned him to a restricted service role.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Pearl Harbor turned routine duty into crisis.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He carried wounded shipmates to safety.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He operated a weapon he had not been trained to use.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- His courage earned the Navy Cross.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Recognition came before equal opportunity did.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- A title cannot contain the full\nmeasure of your ability.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Segregated recruitment and mess attendant uniform.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Laundry collection interrupted by alarm.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Smoke and damaged deck without graphic imagery.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Miller helping wounded sailors.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Antiaircraft gun silhouette.\n\nSCENE 6 --- Navy Cross and final branded card.",
