    "canonicalStory": "Roy Benavidez grew up orphaned and poor in Texas. During an earlier deployment in Vietnam, he stepped on a land mine and suffered injuries so severe doctors doubted he would walk again. He quietly trained himself, beginning with movement at night, and eventually returned to active duty. In May 1968, Benavidez heard by radio that a Special Forces team was surrounded. He jumped aboard a helicopter carrying only a medical bag and a knife. For hours he moved through intense fire, treating casualties, organizing defense, recovering sensitive documents, and helping load the wounded onto evacuation aircraft. He was wounded repeatedly. Benavidez survived and eventually received the Medal of Honor. The inspirational center is not invincibility. He needed a team, evacuation crews, medical care, and years of advocacy before his actions were fully recognized. His courage was the repeated decision to make the next useful move for someone else.",
    "decision": "He decided that hearing the call for help was enough reason to move toward it.",
    "reelspiration": "When the whole problem is too large, courage can be the next useful action taken for another person.",
    "principle": "Extreme endurance is built from purpose, training, teamwork, and one decision repeated under pressure.",
    "nextStep": "In a difficult situation, stop asking how to solve everything. Identify the next useful action and complete it.",
    "whyItMatters": "Benavidez helped save members of a trapped team and became an enduring example of rescue-centered courage and perseverance after catastrophic injury.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Roy Benavidez had once been told he might never walk normally again\nafter a land-mine injury. He fought his way back to service. Then, in\n1968, he heard that a Special Forces team was trapped. He boarded a\nhelicopter with a medical bag and moved into the fight. For hours,\ndespite repeated wounds, he treated casualties, organized defense,\nrecovered documents, and helped evacuate the team. Here is today's\nReelspiration: When the whole problem is too large, courage can be the\nnext useful action taken for another person.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- Doctors doubted he would walk normally again.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- He trained his way back to active duty.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- A radio call reported a team trapped under fire.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Benavidez boarded a helicopter with a medical bag.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He kept treating, organizing, and evacuating despite\nrepeated wounds.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- His courage was a chain of useful decisions.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Take the next useful action for\nsomeone else.",
    "visualDirection": "SCENE 1 --- Hospital rehabilitation and first steps.\n\nSCENE 2 --- Radio call in a tense operations room.\n\nSCENE 3 --- Helicopter departure.\n\nSCENE 4 --- Rescue sequence focused on aid, not violence.\n\nSCENE 5 --- Evacuation and medical care.\n\nSCENE 6 --- End card.",
