    "reelspiration": "A changed body or changed plan does not end your ability to contribute. Sometimes purpose survives by taking a new form.",
    "principle": "Identity can be rebuilt around values rather than a single role or physical ability.",
    "nextStep": "Name the value underneath a role you lost. Find one new way to express that value now.",
    "whyItMatters": "Duckworth transformed combat recovery into sustained public service and advocacy for veterans, military families, and people with disabilities.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Tammy Duckworth was flying a Black Hawk in Iraq when a rocket-propelled\ngrenade struck her helicopter. She lost both legs and partial use of her\nright arm. Recovery required surgeries, rehabilitation, prosthetics, and\nthe support of the crew and medical teams who saved her. Duckworth could\nno longer serve exactly as she had before. So service took another form.\nShe led veterans' programs, joined the Department of Veterans Affairs,\nand entered Congress. Here is today's Reelspiration: A changed body or\nchanged plan does not end your ability to contribute. Purpose can\nsurvive by taking a new form.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- She flew Black Hawk helicopters in Iraq.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- An attack changed her body and expected future.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Her crew and medical teams helped save her life.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Recovery required learning daily life again.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- She turned toward veterans' advocacy and public service.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- The role changed. The value beneath it remained.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Purpose can survive by taking a new\nform.",
