    "canonicalStory": "Witold Pilecki was a Polish cavalry officer and resistance member after Germany invaded Poland. In 1940, he volunteered for a plan to enter Auschwitz under an assumed identity, organize resistance, and send reports to the outside world. Inside the camp, Pilecki helped build a clandestine network, gathered information about mass murder, and transmitted reports through escapees and resistance channels. He endured starvation, violence, disease, and the constant threat of execution before escaping in 1943. After the war he collected intelligence on the Soviet-backed government in Poland. Arrested by communist authorities, he was tortured, subjected to a show trial, and executed in 1948. His story demonstrates both the power and limits of testimony: telling the truth does not guarantee that institutions will act in time---but silence guarantees even less.",
    "decision": "He decided that documenting the crime was worth entering the place designed to hide it.",
    "reelspiration": "One witness may not be able to stop an entire system. But truth still needs someone willing to carry it out.",
    "principle": "Moral courage often begins with creating a record that power wants erased.",
    "nextStep": "Record one important truth accurately---especially where confusion or silence protects harm.",
    "whyItMatters": "Pilecki's reports became among the earliest detailed intelligence from Auschwitz and remain evidence of organized resistance inside the camp.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Witold Pilecki was a Polish resistance officer who volunteered to be\ncaptured and sent to Auschwitz. His mission was to organize resistance\nand report what was happening inside. He built a clandestine network,\ngathered evidence of mass murder, and sent information to the outside\nworld before escaping in 1943. After the war, a communist regime\narrested, tortured, and executed him. Here is today's Reelspiration: One\nwitness may not be able to stop an entire system. But truth still needs\nsomeone willing to carry it out.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He volunteered for a mission inside Auschwitz.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- His purpose was resistance and documentation.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- He built a clandestine network among prisoners.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Reports carried evidence beyond the camp.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- He escaped---but later faced another repressive state.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Truth did not protect him, but his record survived.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Truth still needs a witness willing to\ncarry it.",
