    "canonicalStory": "Born in New Zealand and raised in Australia, Nancy Wake was working in France when World War II began. After Germany occupied the country, she became involved in escape networks that helped Allied personnel and others cross borders to safety. The Gestapo pursued her and reportedly considered her a high-priority target. After several attempts, Wake escaped through Spain and reached Britain. She joined the Special Operations Executive, trained in communications, weapons, and clandestine work, then parachuted back into France in 1944. She coordinated supplies and communication among resistance groups before and during the liberation campaign. Wake's story was costly. Her husband, Henri Fiocca, was captured, tortured, and killed without revealing her location. Her courage should not be packaged as glamorous espionage. It was adaptation under occupation, sustained by networks, and shadowed by grief.",
