    "canonicalStory": "Tibor Rubin was born in Hungary and survived imprisonment in the Mauthausen concentration camp during the Holocaust. After emigrating to the United States, he joined the Army and served in the Korean War. In combat, Rubin repeatedly exposed himself to danger to protect his unit. Captured by Chinese forces, he spent more than two years as a prisoner of war. Fellow prisoners later described how he stole food from enemy stores, prepared meals, cared for the sick, and used knowledge from earlier imprisonment to sustain morale and survival. Rubin's Medal of Honor was awarded in 2005, decades after the war. Reviews found that prejudice within the military may have obstructed earlier recognition. His story is not simply endurance through two systems of captivity. It is the decision to use painful knowledge to protect others rather than retreat entirely into self-preservation.",
