    "canonicalStory": "Eugene Bullard left the segregated United States as a teenager and eventually settled in Europe. When World War I began, he joined the French Foreign Legion, was wounded at Verdun, and later trained as a pilot in the French air service. Bullard flew combat missions over the Western Front, becoming the first Black American military pilot. When the United States entered the war, he sought transfer to American service but was not accepted as a pilot. His achievement existed even when his home country's military structure refused to recognize it. He later aided France again during World War II and returned to the United States, where he lived in relative obscurity for years. Recognition came slowly and largely after the fact. Bullard's story asks a difficult question: how much talent does society lose when opportunity is rationed by prejudice?",
