    "canonicalStory": "Mary Edwards Walker earned a medical degree in 1855, entering a profession that treated women as outsiders. During the Civil War, she sought a commission as an Army surgeon but initially served as an unpaid volunteer in field hospitals and near battle lines. Walker eventually worked as a contract surgeon. She crossed lines to treat civilians and was captured by Confederate forces, spending months as a prisoner of war. In 1865, she received the Medal of Honor for her wartime service---the only woman ever awarded the medal. A later review rescinded hundreds of medals, including Walker's, though she continued wearing hers. The award was restored in 1977. She also campaigned for women's suffrage and dress reform. Her life illustrates that systems often depend on work they are unwilling to honor equally.",
    "decision": "She decided to practice medicine where people needed it, even without the rank, pay, or acceptance given to male physicians.",
    "reelspiration": "Necessary work does not become less valuable because recognition arrives late---or is taken away.",
