    "canonicalStory": "Charles McGee joined the Tuskegee Airmen during World War II and trained at a time when racist assumptions were built into American military policy. He flew escort and combat missions in Europe as part of a group whose performance challenged claims that Black pilots lacked the ability for military aviation. McGee continued serving through the Korean and Vietnam wars, eventually flying more than four hundred combat missions. His longevity was not merely a personal record. It placed Black professional excellence in spaces where exclusion had long been defended as policy. The Tuskegee Airmen did not end racism by proving themselves. The burden should never have been theirs. But their record helped weaken the official arguments used to maintain segregation, and President Truman ordered desegregation of the armed forces in 1948. McGee's life shows endurance without pretending that excellence should be required to earn equality.",
    "decision": "He decided to keep flying and leading without accepting segregation's judgment of his worth.",
    "reelspiration": "Excellence can expose a false barrier---but human dignity should never have depended on passing the barrier's test.",
