    "nextStep": "Maintain your standard, but also name the unfair standard you should never have been required to overcome.",
    "whyItMatters": "McGee's record helped demonstrate the skill and leadership of Black military aviators and contributed to the broader history surrounding military desegregation.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Charles McGee began flying with the Tuskegee Airmen while the U.S.\nmilitary was segregated. He flew combat missions in World War II, then\ncontinued through Korea and Vietnam---more than four hundred combat\nmissions across three wars. His record challenged racist claims used to\nexclude Black pilots. But the lesson is not that people must prove\nextraordinary excellence to deserve equality. Here is today's\nReelspiration: Excellence can expose a false barrier---but dignity\nshould never have depended on passing the barrier's test.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- He trained in a segregated military.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- The Tuskegee Airmen faced doubts built into official policy.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- McGee flew combat missions in Europe.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- He continued serving through Korea and Vietnam.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- His excellence challenged false assumptions.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Equality should never have required extraordinary proof.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Expose the false barrier, then work to\nremove it.",
