    "canonicalStory": "At nineteen, Amy Purdy developed meningococcal meningitis and septic shock. Her survival required intensive medical care, and both legs were amputated below the knee. She also lost kidney function and later received a kidney transplant from her father. Purdy has described the early fear of never snowboarding again. Standard prosthetic feet were not designed for the movement she wanted, so she and others experimented with equipment and technique. She returned to snowboarding, competed internationally, and became a Paralympic medalist. The responsible lesson is not that optimism guarantees recovery. Purdy survived because of medical teams, donors, family support, rehabilitation, technology, and her own sustained work. Her story is about creative adaptation: when the old path disappeared, she began asking what could be built for the new terrain.",
