    "canonicalStory": "Growing up in Pakistan's Swat Valley, Malala Yousafzai spoke publicly and wrote about the right of girls to attend school as the Taliban imposed restrictions and destroyed schools. In October 2012, a gunman boarded her school bus and shot her. Emergency treatment in Pakistan and specialized care in the United Kingdom saved her life. Recovery included surgeries, rehabilitation, and adapting to lasting effects. The world's attention turned toward her, but international fame did not erase the trauma, displacement, or danger surrounding her family. Malala continued advocating for education and co-founded Malala Fund. She became the youngest Nobel Peace Prize laureate. The lesson is not that surviving violence automatically makes someone stronger. It is that an attempt to silence one voice can fail when the person, community, and institutions around that voice help it continue.",
    "decision": "She decided that the attack would not be allowed to define the limits of her advocacy.",
    "reelspiration": "What was meant to silence you does not automatically own the next sentence.",
    "principle": "Recovery can restore agency: not the obligation to speak, but the right to decide whether and how to continue.",
    "nextStep": "Write the next honest sentence about something fear has made you avoid saying.",
    "whyItMatters": "Malala's survival and advocacy expanded global attention and funding for girls' education while connecting her story to millions of less-visible students and activists.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "Malala Yousafzai spoke for girls' education as extremists closed schools\nin Pakistan's Swat Valley. In 2012, a gunman boarded her school bus and\nshot her. Emergency medicine and rehabilitation saved her life. The\nattack was intended to silence her. Instead, Malala chose to continue\nspeaking, co-founded Malala Fund, and became the youngest Nobel Peace\nPrize laureate. Here is today's Reelspiration: What was meant to silence\nyou does not automatically own the next sentence.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- She spoke publicly for girls' education.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- A gunman attacked her on the school bus.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- Medical teams in Pakistan and Britain helped save her life.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- Recovery included surgery, rehabilitation, and displacement.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- She chose to continue advocating.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Her voice became connected to millions of other students.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Fear does not automatically own your\nnext sentence.",
