    "canonicalStory": "In October 1972, a chartered aircraft carrying a Uruguayan rugby team and others crashed in the Andes. Nando Parrado survived despite a skull fracture and days of unconsciousness. His mother and sister died. The survivors faced freezing temperatures, little food, avalanche danger, and no knowledge that official searches had ended. After more than two months, Parrado and Roberto Canessa began a trek across the mountains to find help. They lacked proper climbing equipment and initially misunderstood the scale of the range. After days of climbing and walking, they reached a Chilean herder who helped alert authorities. Rescue followed for the remaining survivors. The story includes morally complex survival choices and immense trauma. It should not be turned into an adventure fantasy. Its central insight is decision-making under uncertainty: after careful preparation, the survivors concluded that remaining still had become the greater risk.",
    "decision": "He decided to leave the crash site when waiting for rescue had become less viable than seeking it.",
    "reelspiration": "There are moments when safety is no longer the place you are standing; it is the direction you must begin moving.",
    "principle": "Courage under uncertainty means comparing risks honestly, preparing carefully, and acting without guarantees.",
    "nextStep": "Name the cost of staying exactly where you are---not only the risk of moving.",
    "whyItMatters": "Parrado and Canessa's expedition made the rescue of sixteen survivors possible and became a major case study in endurance, cooperation, and survival decision-making.",
    "reelScriptAuthored": "A plane carrying a Uruguayan rugby team crashed high in the Andes in\n1972. Nando Parrado survived a skull fracture, then learned his mother\nand sister had died. The survivors endured cold, hunger, and an\navalanche---and did not know the official search had ended. After more\nthan two months, Parrado and Roberto Canessa began walking across the\nmountains for help. They had no guarantee they were heading toward\nrescue. Here is today's Reelspiration: There are moments when safety is\nno longer the place you are standing; it is the direction you must begin\nmoving.",
    "carouselAuthored": "SLIDE 1 --- The aircraft crashed high in the Andes.\n\nSLIDE 2 --- Parrado survived injury and devastating loss.\n\nSLIDE 3 --- The group endured cold, hunger, and an avalanche.\n\nSLIDE 4 --- They learned that waiting might no longer bring rescue.\n\nSLIDE 5 --- Parrado and Canessa walked toward uncertain help.\n\nSLIDE 6 --- Their movement opened the way to rescue sixteen survivors.\n\nSLIDE 7 --- THE REELSPIRATION --- Sometimes safety becomes a direction,\nnot a place.",
