    "canonicalStory": "On Christmas Eve 1971, seventeen-year-old Juliane Koepcke was aboard LANSA Flight 508 over Peru when the aircraft broke apart during a storm. Still strapped to a row of seats, she fell into the rainforest and survived with injuries. Alone, without glasses and with limited supplies, Koepcke relied on knowledge from her biologist parents: small streams feed larger streams, and settlements are often found near waterways. She followed water through the forest for days, dealing with wounds, insects, and exhaustion until she found a boat and shelter used by local workers. Koepcke was eventually rescued and later became a biologist. Her story is extraordinary, but it should not become a myth of individual self-sufficiency. Scientific knowledge, local workers, medical care, and prior family experience all formed the chain that brought her home.",
    "decision": "She decided to follow the water rather than wander without a reference point.",
    "reelspiration": "When you cannot see the whole route, follow the most reliable principle you still know.",
