    "canonicalStory": "Alex Zanardi competed in Formula One and became a champion in American open-wheel racing. In 2001, a high-speed crash in Germany caused catastrophic blood loss and resulted in the amputation of both legs. Emergency teams saved his life. Zanardi returned to driving using adapted controls, then became an elite handcyclist and Paralympic champion. Engineering curiosity was central to his process: he worked with equipment, training, and body mechanics rather than trying to recreate his former life exactly. In 2020, Zanardi suffered severe brain and facial injuries in a handcycling collision and entered another prolonged medical journey. His story must not be frozen at the first comeback or used to demand endless positivity. A life can contain multiple rebuilds, and dignity does not depend on returning to competition.",
