    "canonicalStory": "Randy Pausch was a computer science professor at Carnegie Mellon University when he was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. After treatment, he learned the disease had returned and that his remaining time was likely limited. Invited to give a \"last lecture,\" Pausch spoke about achieving childhood dreams, enabling the dreams of others, and the practical lessons he had learned. The lecture was energetic and humorous, but its purpose was deeply personal: a way to leave his children a record of his voice, values, and approach to life. Pausch died in 2008. His story should never imply that attitude determines cancer outcomes or that dying can be transformed into a tidy success. The inspiration lies in authorship: when he could not control the length of the remaining chapter, he became deliberate about what it would contain.",
