    "canonicalStory": "As a teenage climber, Hugh Herr was caught in a blizzard on Mount Washington in 1982. Exposure and frostbite led to the amputation of both legs below the knee. A rescuer died during the search, a loss that remains part of the event and should not be omitted from a triumphant innovation story. Herr returned to climbing using prosthetic limbs he modified for different terrain. He discovered that an artificial limb did not need to imitate a biological leg in every way; it could be designed for the specific task. That experience drove him toward engineering and research. At MIT, Herr became a leader in biomechatronics and advanced prosthetic systems. His work reflects a shift from viewing disability only as a medical deficit toward examining how technology and environments can be redesigned. The story is not that engineering \"fixes\" a person. It is that users of technology often see possibilities designers have overlooked.",
    "decision": "He decided not to accept existing prosthetics as the final definition of what movement could be.",
    "reelspiration": "The person living with the limitation may also be the person best positioned to redesign the tool.",
