    "canonicalStory": "Tiger Woods became one of the most dominant athletes in modern history, winning with a combination of precision, intensity, and expectations few people ever carry. Success made him famous around the world---but fame also made every failure public. Personal scandal damaged his reputation, repeated back surgeries threatened his ability to walk and compete, and his ranking collapsed. The question was no longer whether he could dominate golf. It was whether he could play meaningful golf again. Woods rebuilt slowly. Rehabilitation replaced headlines. Small tournaments replaced major victories. He adjusted his swing, managed pain, accepted a different body, and kept returning without any guarantee that the old results would follow. At the 2019 Masters, Woods won his fifth green jacket and first major championship in eleven years. The victory did not erase the years before it. It gave them a different ending. A comeback is not pretending the fall never happened. It is accepting responsibility, adapting to reality, and doing the work required to become trustworthy and capable again.",
