    "canonicalStory": "Apollo 13 launched in April 1970 with the goal of landing on the Moon. The crew, flight controllers, engineers, and contractors had rehearsed countless problems---but not the exact chain of failures that followed. The explosion damaged the spacecraft, forced the Moon landing to be abandoned, and left limited power, water, and life-support capacity. Small errors could become fatal. NASA and the crew converted the lunar module into a lifeboat, improvised carbon-dioxide filtration, rationed resources, and solved one problem after another through disciplined communication. Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, and Fred Haise returned safely to Earth. The mission became proof that success can mean abandoning the original goal in order to protect the people the goal was meant to serve.",
